<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tarikh al-Sudan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1655</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tarikh al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> is a chronicle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a> written in Arabic at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a> by Abd al-Rahman al-Sadi and completed around 1655. It records the history of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhai-empire">Songhai Empire</a>, the Moroccan conquest of 1591 and the rule of the pashas, as well as the lives of the scholars of Timbuktu and Djenne. With the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarikh-al-fattash">Tarikh al-Fattash</a>, it is the most important local source for the history of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-bend">Niger bend</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tarikh-al-sudan.jpg" alt="Tarikh al-Sudan" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: إيان · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D8%AA%D8%A7%D8%B1%D9%8A%D8%AE_%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B3%D9%88%D8%AF%D8%A7%D9%86.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…survive in private family libraries in modern Mali, represents one of the most substantial bodies of pre-colonial African scholarship, covering Islamic jurisprudence, astronomy, mathematics, medicine and history; the great Timbuktu chronicles, the <b>Tarikh al-Sudan</b> and Tarikh al-Fattash, written in the seventeenth century, preserve the memory of Songhai itself.</blockquote><a href="/empire/songhai-empire">Songhai Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Visitors described a fertile, crowded land, and the <b>Tarikh al-Sudan</b>, written in the 17th century, claimed that the villages around Djenné stood so close that a message could be shouted from one to the next.</blockquote><a href="/societies/mali-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Mali Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>Timbuktu remained a centre of scholarship, and local historians wrote the <b>Tarikh al-Sudan</b> and other chronicles in this period.</blockquote><a href="/state/pashalik-of-timbuktu">Pashalik of Timbuktu</a></li></ul></section>
